The Grow Zambia Agenda is now colliding with fiscal reality. As President Hakainde Hichilema seeks parliamentary approval to restructure the machinery of government, a sharp analysis of the financing arithmetic behind his 2031 targets lands alongside a K19.9 billion supplementary budget and a fresh IMF programme. Meanwhile, the Copper Princesses' World Cup exit prompts familiar questions about exposure, and the Jay Jay Banda saga continues to test the line between security and the rule of law.

## Main Stories

1. **HH seeks parliamentary approval to merge, dissolve ministries**
President Hichilema has written to Parliament under Article 92(2)(d) seeking approval to realign government ministries through establishment, merger and dissolution, with Vice-President Mutale-Nalumango set to move the motion. State House says the aim is efficiency and full support for the Grow Zambia Agenda. The critical gap: neither the State House statement nor the reporting names which ministries are to be created, merged or scrapped — a restructuring of this scale being sought without the public knowing the shape of it is a serious transparency deficit.

2. **Can fiscal discipline and Grow Zambia's ambitions coexist?**
A detailed analysis questions how the 10-10-5-3-3-1-1-1 targets will be financed, noting the K19.9 billion supplementary budget (with K18 billion in additional domestic borrowing) arrived just 13 days after a staff-level IMF agreement envisaging a 3% of GDP primary surplus by 2029 — neither yet approved. It makes a sharp distinction between production targets and financing plans, and flags the FRA's purchase of 2.7 million tonnes of maize against an initial 500,000-tonne target, with K14.7 billion needed to pay farmers. This is the most substantive accountability reporting in the batch; the unanswered question is whether the borrowing is a one-off or a pattern.

3. **Jay Jay forfeited his rights by spreading hatred – Wynter**
Former Justice minister Wynter Kabimba argues Emmanuel Jay Banda lost his constitutional protection through seditious statements made abroad, and defends the unconventional methods used to bring him back from Mozambique. A consortium of local and regional civil society organisations has called on Zambia and Mozambique to account for the cross-border apprehension. This is now a recurring story — the fourth instalment in roughly a week, following police confirmation of the capture, Given Lubinda's call for due process, and public defences of the arrest. The reporting still does not disclose the charges Banda faces or when he will appear in court, which is precisely the due-process question Lubinda raised.

4. **Catholic priest questions criteria for retiring civil servants**
Fr Augustine Mwewa has questioned the criteria behind retirements "in national interest," claiming they are politically motivated, after Police PRO Godfrey Chilabi confirmed his own retirement without giving reasons. UPND National Youth Chairperson Gilbert Liswaniso alleged the retirees were working with the opposition. The "national interest" label is being applied without published criteria or reasons — a mechanism that, left unexplained, is indistinguishable from political cleansing.

5. **Copper Princesses need more exposure to compete at WC level – Kanyemba**
Zambia exited the FIFA U17 Women's World Cup after a 1-0 defeat to France, having lost all three Group D games to Japan, Venezuela and France. Coach Carol Kanyemba praised her players' resilience and called for greater international exposure. This is the third instalment in a week tracking the same campaign — from pre-tournament optimism, through the Japan and Venezuela defeats, to elimination. The pattern is consistent: a coach finding positives in defeat while the structural gap against non-African opposition remains unaddressed.

6. **Blades humble Kalampa, as Chirundu stun Maite**
Konkola Blades beat Nkana 2-0 at Konkola Stadium through Emmanuel Kunda and former Nkana striker Idris Ilunga Mbombo, while Chirundu United's 1-0 win over Mufulira Wanderers continued their impressive debut season. This follows last week's report of Nkana reclaiming top spot and, two weeks ago, Blades stunning Zanaco — the Transitional League is producing genuine upsets, though the paywalled report leaves the full standings unclear.

7. **I beat Mposha fair and square – Malambo East MP**
Independent MP Faith Lungu says she beat former Munali UPND MP Mike Mposha fairly, securing 7,118 votes to his 5,091, and warns his claim to have won could suggest the Electoral Commission of Zambia is compromised. Mposha says he declined to petition to avoid dividing the constituency. The vote figures are at least disclosed here — a welcome contrast to the vaguer petition filings elsewhere — but Mposha's decision not to challenge while asserting he won leaves an unresolved contradiction.

## Other Notable Stories

**Economy & Revenue:**
* Coca-Cola's system contributed USD 3.6 billion to COMESA's combined GDP in 2024 (0.3% of the total) and supported over 458,200 jobs, per a Steward Redqueen study presented at the 19th COMESA Business Forum. Note this is a company-commissioned study — treat the "28 jobs per direct job" multiplier as advocacy, not independent audit.
* ZRA has set a K185 billion revenue collection target for 2026 and urged journalists to explain tax policy and scrutinise public institutions, while cautioning against conflating targets, assessments and actual collections. The 2027–2031 Medium-Term Revenue Strategic Framework is the document to watch.

**Governance & Society:**
* Lusaka Mayor Chitambala Mwewa met Busan Vice Mayor Oh Seok Keun to discuss a sister-city partnership on waste management, with Lusaka seeking Korean expertise and investment to convert waste into energy. Aspirational for now — no timeline, no funding commitment.
* The University Teaching Hospitals received 110 wheelchairs from the Dorcas Society of the Woodlands Conference of the Seventh-day Adventist Church, split between the Adult Hospital and four sister hospitals.
* Youth MP Dr Vincent Musilikani criticised Zambia Must Prosper leader Kelvin Fube for opposing student nominations to Parliament, calling it selfish. This follows Fube's "ukutumpika abana" remarks last week — a genuine ongoing debate about youth representation, not a one-off spat.

**Independence & National:**
* Zambia marked its 62nd Independence Day under the theme "Our journey, our growth, our future," with First Lady Mutinta Hichilema calling for unity and Vice-President Mutale-Nalumango describing nation-building as a collective responsibility. Twenty people received national honours at State House. This is the latest in a run of independence-related coverage, consistent with the calendar rather than a new development.

**Media & Commerce:**
* Airtel Zambia launched its Bundle Bonanza promotion via a live-streamed event. This follows the Airtel Africa Tech Fellowship launch a month ago and Zamtel's Super App unveiling three weeks ago — a steady stream of telco promotional events, all essentially marketing.

## Key Takeaways & Watchpoints

* **The ministry realignment motion:** Parliament must demand the full list of ministries to be created, merged or dissolved before approving the motion. A restructuring sought under Article 92(2)(d) without public disclosure of its scope is a test of whether this Parliament scrutinises or simply rubber-stamps.
* **The financing arithmetic:** Watch for final approval of the IMF programme and the K19.9 billion supplementary budget, and whether the K18 billion in additional domestic borrowing is a one-off. The FRA's 2.7 million-tonne maize purchase and its K14.7 billion farmer payment bill are the first real test of whether Grow Zambia's targets have financing behind them.
* **Due process for Jay Jay Banda and the "national interest" retirees:** The government should disclose the charges against Banda and the criteria for retiring civil servants in national interest. Without published reasons, both mechanisms risk being seen as tools of political control rather than security or efficiency.